'''''Pittosporum heterophyllum''''', commonly known as '''Chinese Pittosporum''', is a species of plant in the genus ''[[Pittosporum]]''.<ref>{{cite POWO |id=684498-1 |title=Pittosporum heterophyllum }}</ref> Native to China and Tibet, it is a [[broadleaf evergreen]] shrub that grows to a maximum of {{convert|8-10|feet}} tall.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Dirr |first=Michael A. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_5rICwAAQBAJ |title=Dirr's Encyclopedia of Trees and Shrubs |date=2016-03-17 |publisher=Timber Press |isbn=978-1-60469-743-8 |pages=608 |language=en}}</ref> It has long been grown in gardens ornamentally,<ref>{{Cite journal |date=1901 |title=New Garden Plants of the Year 1900 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/4111301 |journal=Bulletin of Miscellaneous Information (Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew) |volume=1901 |pages=84–99 |jstor=4111301 |issn=0366-4457}}</ref>  especially as a [[hedge]] or screen, in temperate gardens for its densely packed green foliage and the fragrant white to yellow flowers it produces in spring.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pittosporum heterophyllum {{!}} Landscape Plants {{!}} Oregon State University |url=https://landscapeplants.oregonstate.edu/plants/pittosporum-heterophyllum |access-date=2022-08-19 |website=landscapeplants.oregonstate.edu}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Pittosporum heterophyllum (Chinese Pittosporum) {{!}} North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/pittosporum-heterophyllum/ |access-date=2022-08-19 |website=plants.ces.ncsu.edu}}</ref> As a landscaping plant, it is known for being more cold hardy than ''[[Pittosporum tobira]]'' and can be grown in [[hardiness zone]]s 7-9 on the USDA scale.<ref name=":0" />

